    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice.\n2. Calculate the number of favorable outcomes where all four dice show the same number.\n3. Subtract the number of favorable outcomes from the total number of outcomes to get the number of outcomes where not all dice show the same number.\n4. Calculate the probability as the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total number of possible outcomes.**\n\nEach die has 6 faces, so when rolling four dice, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n\\[\n6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the number of favorable outcomes where all four dice show the same number.**\n\nThere are 6 possible outcomes where all four dice show the same number (one for each face of the die). So, there are 6 favorable outcomes.\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the number of outcomes where not all dice show the same number.**\n\nTo find the number of outcomes where not all dice show the same number, we subtract the number of outcomes where all dice show the same number from the total number of outcomes:\n\\[\n1296 - 6 = 1290\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the probability.**\n\nThe probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{215}{216}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
