      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, and since the dice are rolled independently, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6^4 = 1296\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of favorable outcomes where not all dice show the same number:**\n   - First, calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number. Since there are 6 possible numbers (1 through 6), there are 6 such outcomes.\n   - Next, calculate the number of outcomes where not all dice show the same number. This is the total number of outcomes minus the number of outcomes where all dice show the same number:\n   \\[\n   1296 - 6 = 1290\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability:**\n   The probability that not all four dice show the same number is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{215}{216}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
